# SUPERSEDED (2026-10-23, D22: there is no public beta, no /beta and no beta. host). The public front door is now
# deploy/frontdoor/ (template, render.sh, check.sh, compose.yaml), switched on by deploy/cutover/cutover.sh and rolled
# back by rollback.sh; the operator's side is docs/RUNBOOK.md. This file is kept because deploy/README.md and
# compose.yaml still point to it, and for its notes on legacy's http-level settings and on the three X-Forwarded-For cases
# (section 0), which the new files carry over. The /beta blocks below are not used by anything.
#
# EXAMPLE, not used by anything: how the legacy nginx (container decimer_web-nginx-1, the one on host
# port 8080) can put the new stack on /beta beside the legacy site during the beta. Nothing
# here has been applied. See deploy/README.md, "Before /beta" and "Running beside legacy".
#
# What was read from the legacy configuration (docker/nginx/nginx.conf and default.conf, and
# `nginx -T` of the running container, 2026-10-22), and what it means for these blocks:
#   * http level: proxy_read_timeout, proxy_connect_timeout and proxy_send_timeout are 300 s, and the
#     blocks below inherit them. They are overridden here (65 s: just above the edge's 60 s, so the
#     edge's own answer for a slow request arrives first).
#   * access_log /var/log/nginx/access.log at http and server level: it records the client address as
#     nginx sees it (the campus proxy's) and the full URI. For /api/ that URI carries job ids, which are
#     bearer tokens, so the new locations turn the access log off.
#   * regex locations exist (`~ \.php$`, `~ ^/(decimer-segmentation|decimer-ocsr|file-upload)$`): a
#     plain prefix location loses to a matching regex, so the new blocks are `^~`.
#   * there is NO set_real_ip_from / real_ip_header: the legacy nginx sees the campus proxy's address
#     as the client, and its per-IP limits (heavy_req, heavy_conn) count that one address. The blocks
#     below pass the proxy's X-Forwarded-For on; they do not change how legacy counts.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0. Before any of this: who is the client? (TODO Q3, the campus proxy's owner)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The chain is: client -> campus proxy -> legacy nginx -> edge -> api. The edge takes the client from
# X-Forwarded-For, believing only proxies it is told about (TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR in deploy/.env: the
# campus proxy's address(es); compose adds the public network's subnet, which is where the legacy nginx
# will be). The legacy nginx adds what it sees as the peer (the campus proxy) to the right of what it
# received, with $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for. Three cases, to be settled with the proxy's owner BEFORE
# the beta:
#   a. the proxy sends X-Forwarded-For: <client> (appending to or replacing what the client sent):
#      the edge sees "<client>, <campus proxy>"; with the proxy listed in TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR it strips
#      that right-most entry and the client is <client>. This is the wanted case.
#   b. the proxy appends to a client-supplied value without removing it: the client can put anything
#      to the LEFT, but the edge reads from the right and stops at the first address it does not trust,
#      so a forged value is only believed if the proxy itself put it there. Safe, if the proxy's own
#      entry is last.
#   c. the proxy sends no X-Forwarded-For at all: the real client is invisible, every user is the proxy,
#      and the per-IP limits (2 active jobs, rate limits) are shared by the whole campus. Not acceptable
#      for the beta: ask the owner to add the header (or to pass the client address another way).
# How to check, before the first beta user: ask the owner what the proxy does, and verify through the
# real path with a harmless request carrying a made-up header, e.g.
#   curl -s -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.7' https://decimer.ai/beta/readyz
# and look in the API's log (not the edge's, which has no addresses): with the chain working, the API
# does not warn about an untrusted X-Forwarded-For peer, and two different test machines do not share
# one rate limit. If the campus proxy is not yet in TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR, every client looks like the
# proxy; that is safe (nobody can choose an identity) but shared.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Reach the edge (an operation on the RUNNING legacy container: needs the owner's go-ahead)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The edge publishes only 127.0.0.1:8090, which a container cannot reach through the docker gateway.
# Attach the legacy nginx to the `public` network of the new stack (it holds nothing but the edge; the
# legacy nginx then cannot reach the api or anything else of the new stack):
#     docker network connect decimer-next_public decimer_web-nginx-1
# The edge answers to `decimer-next-edge` there. Or have the campus proxy route /beta/ and /api/ to the
# new stack directly (TODO Q3). In both cases the proxy's address goes into TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The blocks, for the legacy server block (docker/nginx/default.conf of DECIMER_Web)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why a resolver and a variable: with `proxy_pass http://decimer-next-edge:8080;` nginx resolves the
# name when it loads its configuration, and REFUSES TO START (or to reload) with "host not found in
# upstream" whenever the edge container is not there: stopped, being recreated, or the network
# disconnected. That would take the legacy site down with the beta. With a variable, the name is
# resolved per request (through docker's resolver, valid on the legacy compose's user-defined network),
# and a missing edge is a 502 on /beta/ and /api/ only.

    resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=10s ipv6=off;
    set $beta_edge http://decimer-next-edge:8080;

    # The prefix is stripped from the RAW request line ($request_uri), so nothing is decoded on the way:
    # no %0d%0a reaches the upstream request line. (At http level, outside the server block:)
    #   map $request_uri $beta_path {
    #       ~^/beta(?<rest>/.*)$  $rest;
    #       default               $request_uri;
    #   }

    # The beta. `^~`: this prefix wins over the legacy regex locations.
    location = /beta { return 301 /beta/; }
    location ^~ /beta/ {
        access_log off;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Connection "";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_connect_timeout 5s;
        proxy_send_timeout 65s;
        proxy_read_timeout 65s;           # just above PROXY_READ_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (60); the http level says 300
        client_max_body_size 101m;        # the edge's limit: MAX_UPLOAD_MB (100) + 1
        proxy_pass $beta_edge$beta_path;
    }

    # The API answers with ABSOLUTE urls ("cropUrl": "/api/jobs/<id>/media/crop-0.png", header
    # "Location: /api/jobs/<id>"), so /api/ itself has to reach the new stack, not just /beta/. The
    # legacy site has no /api/ routes that matter (routes/api.php has only the unused /api/user).
    location ^~ /api/ {
        access_log off;                   # job ids in the URL are bearer tokens
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Connection "";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_connect_timeout 5s;
        proxy_send_timeout 65s;
        proxy_read_timeout 65s;
        client_max_body_size 101m;
        proxy_pass $beta_edge$request_uri;
    }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Order of operations. The legacy site is the thing that must not break.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apply:
#   1. new stack up and healthy ($DC ps, curl 127.0.0.1:8090/readyz);
#   2. docker network connect decimer-next_public decimer_web-nginx-1;
#   3. add the blocks above to a COPY of default.conf, test it in a throwaway container
#      (docker run --rm -v <copy>:/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf:ro ... nginx -t), then put it in place
#      and `nginx -s reload` in the legacy container. A reload that fails leaves the old configuration
#      running; a reload with the variable form cannot fail because of the edge.
# Roll back, in THIS order (and the reverse of apply):
#   1. restore the previous default.conf (kept) and reload the legacy nginx: /beta/ and /api/ are gone;
#   2. docker network disconnect decimer-next_public decimer_web-nginx-1;
#   3. only then stop the new stack ($DC down).
# With the variable form the order does not break legacy either way; with a literal proxy_pass host it
# would (the legacy nginx could not reload, or restart, once the edge is gone).
#
# Cutover is the other way round: `location /` goes to the edge (the legacy `location /` block is
# kept, commented, as the rollback), and /beta/ can be dropped or redirected.
